course: dictionary
lesson: 13576
title: Incapacity: when you cannot do something
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Incapacity is a noun. It names the state of not being able to do something, usually because of illness, injury or weakness. The related word incapable means unable, and it is an adjective. «Her injury gave her an incapacity to lift heavy bags.» The injury is the clue: a hurt body makes lifting hard, so incapacity means being unable to do something.
explain: Look for the reason in the sentence. Words like illness, injury and weakness tell you why someone is unable to do something. «After the flu, Ben had an incapacity to run.» The word flu is the clue, so Ben could not run because he was ill.
explain: In law and money, incapacity has a special meaning: being unable to work because of illness or disability. «Some countries pay incapacity benefit to people who cannot work.» Benefit means money given by the government. Put the two ideas together, and the meaning is clear: money for people who cannot work because of illness.
explain: In formal writing, incapacity can also mean a lack of ability, not just a body problem. «The report named his incapacity to listen as the main problem.» Here nobody is ill. The word means he was not able to listen well. Check with the swap test: put «inability» into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: Incapacity is a noun, so it names a state or quality.
fact: Incapacity means not being able to do something, especially because of illness, injury or weakness.
fact: In law and money, incapacity means being unable to work because of illness or disability.
fact: «Incapacity benefit» is money paid by some countries to people who cannot work because of illness or disability.
fact: The phrases «incapacity to work» and «mental incapacity» are used in adult life and law.
exercise: What does incapacity mean? | Being very good at sport and fast on your feet | *Not being able to do something, because of illness, injury or weakness | A kind of small bird that cannot fly | Having lots of energy and plenty of time
exercise: «Her injury gave her an incapacity to lift heavy bags.» What caused the incapacity? | *Her injury | Her new coat | Her happy mood | The bright sunshine
exercise: Incapacity benefit is money paid to people who cannot work. Who is it for? | Runners who win races | People going on holiday abroad | *People who cannot work because of illness or disability | Children who want new school bags
test: «The report named his incapacity to listen as the main problem.» What does incapacity mean in this sentence? | *A lack of ability to listen | A very quiet voice | A fast way of reading | A love of loud music
test: The letter said Ann had an incapacity to work after her illness. What does this tell you? | Ann was on a holiday by the sea | Ann had just won a prize for her work | *Ann was unable to work because she was ill | Ann was working very hard on a new job
exam: Which word is closest in meaning to incapacity? | speed | ability | freedom | *inability
